1
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:11,720
بسم الله والصلاة والسلام على رسول الله

2
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:13,900
وعلى آله وصحبه أجمعين

3
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,760
There are two words for the heart. Can you remind me what they are?

4
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:20,280
((قَلْب)) and ((فُؤَاد)).

5
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:23,150
((قَلْب)) is the normal word for the heart. ((فُؤَاد)) is used when?

6
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:28,330
Some sort of excited situation. Emotional circumstance.

7
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:30,250
You know beautifully in the Quran,

8
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:36,290
Allah keeps track of these things at moments notice. So you find in one aayah of

9
00:00:36,330 --> 00:00:39,460
Surah al-Qasas both of the words used for the heart, at the same time.

10
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:41,070
Allah says,

11
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:49,550
وَأَصۡبَحَ فُؤَادُ أُمِّ مُوسَىٰ فَـٰرِغًا إِن كَادَتۡ لَتُبۡدِی بِهِۦ لَوۡلَاۤ أَن رَّبَطۡنَا عَلَىٰ قَلۡبِهَا لِتَكُونَ مِنَ ٱلۡمُؤۡمِنِینَ

12
00:00:49,630 --> 00:00:52,530
Musa's mother's heart was fleeing,

13
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:56,860
fluttering away, emptied because she had just left her baby in the water

14
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:00,760
and now the baby has gone so far, she can't even see him anymore.

15
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:03,860
So she became almost emotionally paralyzed.

16
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:08,850
And at that moment, the fear, and the grief, and the sadness, and the stress,

17
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:10,260
the overwhelming emotions,

18
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:15,600
the rage of emotions were so many that her heart is not described as just (قَلْب),

19
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:16,360
but as what?

20
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:18,700
(فُؤَاد) - because it's overwhelmed.

21
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:20,120
It's burning.

22
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:24,470
But then Allah says she almost gave up her secret.

23
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:25,800
إِن كَادَتۡ لَتُبۡدِی بِهِ

24
00:01:25,810 --> 00:01:29,310
Meaning she almost ran out of her house running after the basket, saying,

25
00:01:29,310 --> 00:01:30,560
'That's my baby!'

26
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,450
She couldn't help herself,

27
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:35,350
but Allah had to hold her back.

28
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:40,050
And this is important to understand, because for a mother to hold back

29
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:44,360
is actually probably psychologically impossible.

30
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:47,490
It's probably psychologically impossible.

31
00:01:47,490 --> 00:01:49,460
I don't see an explanation of how that can happen.

32
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:52,862
In other words, when a child

33
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:55,160
when you take your child to the store

34
00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:57,560
and your child is holding on to your jilbab

35
00:01:57,560 --> 00:01:59,720
and you're walking and all of a sudden for two seconds,

36
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:01,150
you don't feel the hand anymore.

37
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:03,760
What do you do immediately?

38
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:06,560
"Kareem? Kareem?"

39
00:02:06,770 --> 00:02:07,840
[screams]

40
00:02:07,899 --> 00:02:09,579
[laughs]

41
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:12,660
How long before a mother goes crazy?

42
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:15,070
I literally give it 10 seconds.

43
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:19,450
A father doesn't see the kid anymore. "Eh, he's somewhere."

44
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:21,860
You know, like

45
00:02:23,540 --> 00:02:24,460
"He's around."

46
00:02:25,640 --> 00:02:26,060
You know?

47
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:28,680
But a mother. You give her 10 seconds.

48
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:30,880
And she goes nuts.

49
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:33,230
She goes crazy.

50
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:35,430
Mothers don't care.

51
00:02:35,440 --> 00:02:39,190
There's an animal instinct that takes over that wants to protect the child,

52
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:40,560
and nothing else matters.

53
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:46,250
I've seen cases where there are people speeding on a road, lightly.

54
00:02:46,250 --> 00:02:49,170
Maybe it's 15 miles an hour and they're going 18

55
00:02:49,170 --> 00:02:51,820
miles an hour and there are kids playing in the porch.

56
00:02:51,820 --> 00:02:55,780
But they're not on the street. The mother comes out with a bat. "Slow your car down!"

57
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:57,650
"My Children are playing here." "Oh my God. Lady."

58
00:02:58,240 --> 00:02:59,660
"Whoa, whoa, whoa."

59
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:03,040
Because there's a protective instinct

60
00:03:03,170 --> 00:03:03,950
of a mother.

61
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:06,710
This mother just put her baby

62
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:07,450
in water.

63
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:09,650
What is her first instinct?

64
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:13,250
You think she's sure about what she's doing? Like, "Eh, it's a good idea."

65
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:14,750
"Hi."

66
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:16,050
No.

67
00:03:16,740 --> 00:03:20,760
She wants to drag it back. And now that it's gone, what do I do? She can't sit still.

68
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:24,060
She almost came out of the house and gave up the secret.

69
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:27,786
So something has to hold her back. So Allah says,

70
00:03:27,806 --> 00:03:30,206
لَوۡلَاۤ أَن رَّبَطۡنَا عَلَىٰ قَلۡبِهَا

71
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:32,470
Had We not tied-

72
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:33,760
knotted up

73
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:35,770
on top of her heart.

74
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:40,060
Meaning Allah gave her emotional stillness and calmness

75
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:43,170
and stability so she could not lose it

76
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:47,320
and go after her baby. Because it was important that she stayed calm right now,

77
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:51,120
even though that is psychologically impossible, Allah had to intervene.

78
00:03:51,130 --> 00:03:54,960
So the calmness of her heart did not come from her. It came from Allah.

79
00:03:54,970 --> 00:03:57,560
It had to come from Allah. She wasn't capable of it

80
00:03:57,730 --> 00:03:59,850
on her own it was her (فُؤَاد).

81
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:04,060
Allah came through and calmed her heart down.

82
00:04:04,070 --> 00:04:07,710
And when the heart calms down, and it's not overwhelmed by emotion anymore,

83
00:04:07,710 --> 00:04:10,650
what does it become? (قَلْب). So He says,

84
00:04:10,650 --> 00:04:13,370
لَوۡلَاۤ أَن رَّبَطۡنَا عَلَىٰ قَلۡبِهَا

85
00:04:13,390 --> 00:04:15,050
When it calmed down it became (قَلْب) again.

86
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:20,870
In it there's an incredible lesson also for us in regards to our emotional state.

87
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:24,340
Some people say I have anger issues. There's nothing you can do about it.

88
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:29,030
Other people say "I have, you know, I get really upset. I get really depressed."

89
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:31,260
"What can I do? I can't help it. This is just who I am."

90
00:04:31,940 --> 00:04:35,640
It's not just who you are. And some people also say - this is also interesting -

91
00:04:35,820 --> 00:04:37,360
Some people are scarred.

92
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:41,430
You have a fight with a cousin or a friend or a brother or a sister,

93
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:44,050
and you're like, "Don't even say their name.

94
00:04:44,050 --> 00:04:45,950
Okay? When you say their name, I get upset.

95
00:04:47,240 --> 00:04:48,850
I can't even think about that person.

96
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:50,960
It just makes me angry,

97
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:53,330
you know, talk about something else."

98
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:56,880
You don't want to look at them, talk to them, think about them. Ever.

99
00:04:56,890 --> 00:04:59,190
There are some of you that have that issue with your mother-in-law,

100
00:04:59,190 --> 00:05:00,860
your father-in-law, or somebody.

101
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:03,890
You know? Issues like that happen.

102
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,570
They sometimes even happen between siblings, between friends,

103
00:05:06,570 --> 00:05:07,280
used to be friends,

104
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:08,490
now they hate each other's guts.

105
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:10,050
They can't stand each other

106
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:13,350
and they say, "My heart is scarred

107
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:15,660
and it can't heal.

108
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:20,160
I can't get over it. I cannot forget what happened. I cannot move on.

109
00:05:20,170 --> 00:05:23,820
I know that he's sorry for what he did. I know that he's changed.

110
00:05:23,830 --> 00:05:27,560
I know that everything is okay now, but I cannot let go of what happened."

111
00:05:28,440 --> 00:05:34,340
The idea in the aayah is that the mother of Musa (عليه السلام)  just put her baby in water.

112
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:36,660
And now the baby has disappeared.

113
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:38,560
Which means

114
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:43,550
and as far as she can see, this is never going coming--this baby isn't coming back.

115
00:05:44,110 --> 00:05:44,960
It's gone.

116
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:49,430
This sort of thing. Can this be emotionally traumatizing for a person?

117
00:05:49,550 --> 00:05:50,180
For a woman

118
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:51,860
to have to go through that?

119
00:05:52,940 --> 00:05:56,750
You could say, that after going through something like that, she will never recover.

120
00:05:57,940 --> 00:05:59,050
She will never recover.

121
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:02,560
But Allah intervened and had her

122
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:04,860
had her recover immediately.

123
00:06:05,930 --> 00:06:08,510
Immediately, Allah gave her (رَبْط) on her (قَلْب).

124
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:10,600
Now tell me, is the mother of Musa a prophet?

125
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:12,650
No.

126
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:16,300
Are the People of the Cave prophets?

127
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:24,040
وَرَبَطۡنَا عَلَىٰ قُلُوبِهِمۡ إِذۡ قَامُوا۟ فَقَالُوا۟ رَبُّنَا رَبُّ ٱلسَّمَـٰوَ ٰ⁠تِ وَٱلۡأَرۡضِ

128
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:27,470
Allah doesn't just tie the heart, and calm the heart down, and

129
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:30,600
give the strength to the heart, and heal the heart for prophets.

130
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:33,200
Allah intervenes

131
00:06:33,540 --> 00:06:35,360
between you and your heart.

132
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:37,960
Allah comes into your heart

133
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:40,240
and intervenes and fixes.

134
00:06:41,067 --> 00:06:45,357
وَٱعۡلَمُوۤا۟ أَنَّ ٱللَّهَ یَحُولُ بَیۡنَ ٱلۡمَرۡءِ وَقَلۡبِهِ

135
00:06:45,397 --> 00:06:47,860
Allah says it Himself. He intervenes in your heart.

136
00:06:48,740 --> 00:06:49,360
In other words,

137
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:53,250
Allah has the capacity to move you forward to let you let

138
00:06:53,250 --> 00:06:57,400
go of the past. To heal the previous damage that was done.

139
00:06:57,410 --> 00:06:59,050
And you can live your life.

140
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:00,860
There are some people who, many of you still

141
00:07:00,860 --> 00:07:02,750
sitting in the audience, you're living in the past.

142
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:05,270
Something that happened to you and you can't let go

143
00:07:05,270 --> 00:07:06,810
of it. You think about it all the time and

144
00:07:06,810 --> 00:07:08,720
it scars you even more and you don't let go

145
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:10,770
and you remember it and you get even more depressed.

146
00:07:10,780 --> 00:07:13,260
Husband and wife had a fight six months ago.

147
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:18,160
Everything is okay now. But then one day the wife says, "Remember when we had a fight?"

148
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:23,430
He goes, "Yeah, I know. That was silly."
"No it wasn't silly. It was serious."

149
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:27,690
"I still remember what you said..." and it starts all over again.

150
00:07:27,690 --> 00:07:30,650
He's like, "Wait, this was six months ago. Why are you replaying the video?"

151
00:07:32,140 --> 00:07:35,900
And, it's like, they just fought because she can't let go of what happened.

152
00:07:35,910 --> 00:07:39,760
Even though things worked out, everything was okay, but she's not able to let go.

153
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:42,460
Sometimes the guy is not able to let go.

154
00:07:42,790 --> 00:07:44,294
This is (رَبْطُ الْقَلْب).

155
00:07:44,340 --> 00:07:47,060
Allah can give strength to your heart if you seek it from Him.

156
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:48,540
You know,

157
00:07:48,540 --> 00:07:53,020
when we talk about the discussions about our heart in Islam.

158
00:07:53,030 --> 00:07:57,080
Usually we think about imaan, and taqwaa, and khawf of Allah, and khushoo'

159
00:07:57,170 --> 00:07:58,740
our relationship with Allah.

160
00:07:58,740 --> 00:08:00,680
That's what we think about when we think about the heart.

161
00:08:00,690 --> 00:08:02,360
But actually in the Quran,

162
00:08:02,370 --> 00:08:05,400
the discussion about the heart isn't just about our relationship with

163
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:08,150
Allah. It is also about our relationship with people

164
00:08:09,640 --> 00:08:12,090
because, you know, Allah says on Judgement Day

165
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:14,210
إِلَّا مَنۡ أَتَى ٱللَّهَ بِقَلۡبࣲ سَلِیمࣲ

166
00:08:14,220 --> 00:08:16,300
The only people who will be safe on the Day of

167
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:19,060
Judgment are people who come to Allah with a healthy heart.

168
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:21,660
But my heart doesn't just have

169
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:25,550
a love and a belief and a faith and a fear of Allah.

170
00:08:26,230 --> 00:08:31,351
My heart also has love for my mother, and my wife, and my children, and

171
00:08:31,430 --> 00:08:33,049
everybody else in my life.

172
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:39,240
And if there are fights, and arguments, and hatred, and jealousy, and whatever else,

173
00:08:39,350 --> 00:08:41,860
that's also - where does that stuff live?

174
00:08:42,539 --> 00:08:43,659
In my heart.

175
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:47,440
So if I have feelings towards other people, my heart is not healthy.

176
00:08:48,840 --> 00:08:51,150
If I'm going to come before Allah, I need a healthy heart.

177
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:55,230
Not just a heart that's healthy towards Him. Healthy altogether.

178
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:57,700
Which means my dealings with people need to be good.

179
00:08:58,640 --> 00:08:59,870
They need to be sound.

180
00:08:59,880 --> 00:09:03,168
We need to amend our relationships. That's captured inside:

181
00:09:03,168 --> 00:09:04,998
لَوۡلَاۤ أَن رَّبَطۡنَا عَلَىٰ قَلۡبِهَا

182
00:09:05,018 --> 00:09:05,750
SubhaanAllah.

183
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:06,650
Okay,

184
00:09:07,740 --> 00:09:11,060
I'll give you a couple more quick examples. This is a really fun example.

185
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,202
ٱهۡبِطُوا۟ مِصۡرࣰا فَإِنَّ لَكُم مَّا سَأَلۡتُمۡۗ

186
00:09:16,400 --> 00:09:17,660
Let me write this for you

187
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:19,860
so you can tell the difference.

188
00:09:21,959 --> 00:09:23,650
(مِصۡرࣰا)

189
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:25,839
and (مِصْرَ).

190
00:09:28,040 --> 00:09:29,260
(مِصْرَ)

191
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:33,560
(مِصْرًا)

192
00:09:35,840 --> 00:09:39,760
When you say (مِصْرًا) with a tanween, it actually means countryside,

193
00:09:40,440 --> 00:09:42,860
farmland, etcetera, etcetera.

194
00:09:43,240 --> 00:09:44,640
When you say (مِصْرَ),

195
00:09:45,540 --> 00:09:46,350
it means Egypt.

196
00:09:49,040 --> 00:09:50,310
One little difference.

197
00:09:50,690 --> 00:09:52,830
The difference is "an" versus

198
00:09:52,950 --> 00:09:56,660
"a", the technical discussion here (مَمْنُوع مِنَ الصَّرْف)

199
00:09:57,140 --> 00:09:57,890
versus (مُعْرَب)

200
00:09:57,920 --> 00:10:04,250
Who cares? All it means at the end is (مِصْرًا) is for countryside. (مِصْرَ) is for

201
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:05,530
Egypt.

202
00:10:05,650 --> 00:10:06,350
Now,

203
00:10:07,940 --> 00:10:08,740
Fir'awn says,

204
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:09,520
أَلَیۡسَ لِی

205
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:10,550
Listen to the Arabic.

206
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:12,350
Tell me if it's countryside or Egypt.

207
00:10:12,740 --> 00:10:14,010
Okay, that's your job.

208
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:16,560
أَلَیۡسَ لِی مُلۡكُ مِصۡرَ

209
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:18,960
Egypt.

210
00:10:19,640 --> 00:10:24,050
Don't I alone own the entire Kingdom of Egypt?

211
00:10:25,440 --> 00:10:26,660
Who asked that question?

212
00:10:27,797 --> 00:10:28,061
Fir'awn.

213
00:10:28,061 --> 00:10:30,371
أَلَیۡسَ لِی مُلۡكُ مِصۡرَ

214
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:32,821
وَهَـٰذِهِ ٱلۡأَنۡهَـٰرُ تَجۡرِی مِن تَحۡتِیۤۚ

215
00:10:32,850 --> 00:10:34,660
These rivers flow under my feet!

216
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:36,360
This is my land.

217
00:10:37,340 --> 00:10:40,060
Egypt is mine. That's what he said. Okay.

218
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:41,750
This aayah

219
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:47,160
ٱهۡبِطُوا۟ مِصۡرࣰا فَإِنَّ لَكُم مَّا سَأَلۡتُمۡ

220
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:48,560
مِصۡرࣰا

221
00:10:49,240 --> 00:10:51,160
Was that Egypt or countryside?

222
00:10:51,940 --> 00:10:52,620
Countryside.

223
00:10:52,830 --> 00:10:53,460
Okay,

224
00:10:54,140 --> 00:10:58,960
so now we have to figure out why Musa (عليه السلام) used this word.

225
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:03,950
Musa (عليه السلام) helped the Israelites cross the water.

226
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:05,890
They escaped the clutches of Fir'awn.

227
00:11:05,900 --> 00:11:08,410
They're out in the middle of the desert and

228
00:11:08,410 --> 00:11:10,410
they were going to die because it's a desert.

229
00:11:10,410 --> 00:11:13,580
There's no food. There's no water. So what did Allah do? First of all, Allah

230
00:11:13,580 --> 00:11:16,550
provided them an umbrella. Allah gave them (اَلْغَمَام).

231
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:19,230
The clouds that would follow them everywhere, the Israelites.

232
00:11:19,230 --> 00:11:23,620
So they got a divine umbrella from Allah. So they're not scorched under the sun.

233
00:11:23,630 --> 00:11:28,970
But that's not enough. Now they need water. How did they get water? Anybody know?

234
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:31,260
How did the Israelites get water in the desert?

235
00:11:32,540 --> 00:11:33,160
I'll wait.

236
00:11:33,630 --> 00:11:34,950
Let this get awkward. It's fine.

237
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:39,470
How many springs?

238
00:11:40,440 --> 00:11:43,860
Twelve springs. He hit his staff, twelve springs came out, remember?

239
00:11:44,340 --> 00:11:45,420
Right, so that's water.

240
00:11:45,650 --> 00:11:49,300
Okay, well, water is not enough to survive. We've got cover. We've got water.

241
00:11:49,310 --> 00:11:50,060
What else do you need?

242
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:53,360
Food. How did they get food? What food was it?

243
00:11:54,050 --> 00:11:56,740
(مَنَّ) and (سَلْوَى), right?

244
00:11:56,740 --> 00:11:58,940
وَأَنزَلۡنَا عَلَیۡكُمُ ٱلۡمَنَّ وَٱلسَّلۡوَىٰۖ

245
00:11:58,980 --> 00:12:04,860
(مَنَّ) and (سَلوَى), you can say, it's a kind of seed or grain that they could use to make bread.

246
00:12:05,740 --> 00:12:09,370
And a kind of bird, like a quail kind of bird that actually, some say,

247
00:12:09,370 --> 00:12:11,300
wouldn't fly away when they would try to catch it.

248
00:12:11,310 --> 00:12:14,024
So it was easy to catch. Cook. Eat.

249
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:18,260
So they've got food - they've got bread, and they've got meat.

250
00:12:18,270 --> 00:12:20,770
They've got carbohydrates, and they've got proteins.

251
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:23,660
They're set. And they've got drink. They've got water.

252
00:12:24,540 --> 00:12:27,630
So they're living in the desert, and they're eating (مَنَّ) and (سَلوَى),

253
00:12:27,630 --> 00:12:31,260
And the guy says to his friend, "Hey, what do you want to have for dinner?"

254
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:35,660
"I'm thinking we should have some (مَنَّ) and (سَلوَى) today."

255
00:12:37,540 --> 00:12:41,480
"What about breakfast? What do you want to do for breakfast? I got an idea.

256
00:12:41,490 --> 00:12:43,360
How about (سَلْوَى) and (مَنَّ)?

257
00:12:43,360 --> 00:12:45,860
[crowd laughs]

258
00:12:46,440 --> 00:12:49,760
"I don't know what to do for lunch today."

259
00:12:50,940 --> 00:12:57,080
Breakfast and lunch. Then dinner. (مَنَّ ) and (سَلوَى), then (مَنَّ ) and (سَلوَى), then

260
00:12:57,100 --> 00:13:00,120
(مَنَّ ) and (سَلوَى). Do you have the same breakfast?

261
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:02,120
Same lunch and same dinner every day? You guys?

262
00:13:02,940 --> 00:13:03,360
Do you?

263
00:13:04,540 --> 00:13:07,860
Really, you do? Same exact thing for breakfast and lunch and dinner?

264
00:13:08,740 --> 00:13:09,850
No.

265
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:13,720
As a matter of fact, when you're about to have the same exact meal the second time,

266
00:13:13,720 --> 00:13:16,209
You say, "Hey, I already had this... What?

267
00:13:16,840 --> 00:13:18,050
"What's going on?

268
00:13:18,150 --> 00:13:18,960
Is everything okay?"

269
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:23,590
When the wife gives you lunch and she says, "Can I give you for dinner

270
00:13:23,590 --> 00:13:25,160
the same thing I gave you for lunch?." You're like,

271
00:13:25,740 --> 00:13:26,760
"Excuse me?

272
00:13:27,240 --> 00:13:30,500
Same thing for dinner that I had for lunch - I'm going out to eat!

273
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:33,410
I don't do the same food twice!"

274
00:13:34,500 --> 00:13:37,250
The Israelites are doing the same food three times a day

275
00:13:37,250 --> 00:13:39,150
for months and months and months and months and months.

276
00:13:40,240 --> 00:13:42,000
So eventually one of the guys

277
00:13:43,140 --> 00:13:45,660
he's sitting with his friend and he says, "Hey, man,

278
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:48,360
remember back in the day in Egypt

279
00:13:48,840 --> 00:13:50,460
before we were out in the desert,

280
00:13:51,240 --> 00:13:57,660
we used to have garlic and lentil and onions and tomatoes and cucumbers.

281
00:13:58,040 --> 00:14:00,012
Ohhhhh."

282
00:14:00,440 --> 00:14:02,870
And he's sitting there chewing on his (مَنَّ ) and (سَلوَى) and

283
00:14:02,870 --> 00:14:05,660
he's thinking about the entire salad he used to have.

284
00:14:06,440 --> 00:14:08,960
And he's like, "Wow! And yeah, I remember. Yes.

285
00:14:09,340 --> 00:14:11,050
Mm. Mmhm.

286
00:14:11,740 --> 00:14:12,550
I can taste it.

287
00:14:13,640 --> 00:14:14,960
Hey, let's go ask Musa.

288
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:17,260
He can make dua to Allah.

289
00:14:17,430 --> 00:14:19,310
We've ate enough of this chicken

290
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:23,060
and bread long enough. This fake chicken and bread.

291
00:14:24,140 --> 00:14:28,130
Let's go ask him for some, you know, some side dishes, some salad, some

292
00:14:28,230 --> 00:14:29,150
spices.

293
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:32,190
So they go to Musa (عليه السلام) and they say,

294
00:14:32,190 --> 00:14:40,834
فَٱدۡعُ لَنَا رَبَّكَ یُخۡرِجۡ لَنَا مِمَّا تُنۢبِتُ ٱلۡأَرۡضُ مِنۢ بَقۡلِهَا وَقِثَّاۤىِٕهَا وَفُومِهَا وَعَدَسِهَا وَبَصَلِهَا

295
00:14:40,911 --> 00:14:44,110
Can we get some garlic, and lentil, and onion, and etcetera?

296
00:14:44,110 --> 00:14:47,010
And can we get some fries with that? Some extra ketchup on the side?

297
00:14:47,020 --> 00:14:50,350
I mean, while we're at it. Now the question is,

298
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:54,220
where did they learn the names of these vegetables?

299
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:56,460
Did they learn the names of these vegetables in the desert?

300
00:14:57,340 --> 00:14:58,340
Clearly not.

301
00:14:59,540 --> 00:15:00,260
From Egypt.

302
00:15:00,740 --> 00:15:03,350
They knew these vegetables from Egypt.

303
00:15:04,340 --> 00:15:07,550
So they're asking for food that they used to have in

304
00:15:08,040 --> 00:15:08,660
Egypt.

305
00:15:09,740 --> 00:15:10,830
On a side note,

306
00:15:10,840 --> 00:15:15,260
I recently found out that in Egyptology they studied the food of the elite,

307
00:15:15,740 --> 00:15:20,010
the food of the elite ancient Egyptians, and in their food,

308
00:15:20,020 --> 00:15:23,510
these vegetables that are mentioned in the Quran were actually used

309
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:27,350
in this order of priority and quantity for elite family foods.

310
00:15:27,840 --> 00:15:30,630
So they remember watching rich people eat that food and say, "Man,

311
00:15:30,630 --> 00:15:31,460
I wish I could have that."

312
00:15:32,640 --> 00:15:37,160
That's where this comes from. Anyway. So they asked for these vegetables

313
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:40,450
and Musa (عليه السلام) says to them

314
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:44,650
- before I tell you what he says to them, I need you to understand something further.

315
00:15:45,940 --> 00:15:49,670
So they are missing the food they used to eat in Egypt, yes?

316
00:15:50,440 --> 00:15:52,560
But wasn't Egypt a life of slavery?

317
00:15:53,440 --> 00:15:54,760
Wasn't Egypt jail?

318
00:15:55,540 --> 00:16:00,550
Egypt was jail. When two guys get out of jail and say, "Man, I missed that prison food.

319
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:03,260
That was good food."

320
00:16:05,740 --> 00:16:10,560
When you say I miss prison food, isn't it the same as saying I miss prison?

321
00:16:12,440 --> 00:16:16,740
If you're saying I miss the food I used to eat or look at as a slave,

322
00:16:16,750 --> 00:16:18,450
isn't that the same as saying I miss

323
00:16:19,210 --> 00:16:20,060
slavery?

324
00:16:21,340 --> 00:16:23,460
They go to Musa (عليه السلام), and they say,

325
00:16:24,440 --> 00:16:27,860
"Can we have some of that food we used to have when we were in jail?"

326
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:31,160
Musa (عليه السلام) is so angry at them.

327
00:16:32,640 --> 00:16:34,160
"Are you asking for Egypt?"

328
00:16:35,740 --> 00:16:39,060
He wants to say, "Are you asking me for Egypt all over again?"

329
00:16:39,540 --> 00:16:41,760
But he doesn't. Musa (عليه السلام) is sarcastic.

330
00:16:42,140 --> 00:16:43,970
And he's got an incredible sense of humor here.

331
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:46,100
He's says,
اِهْبِطُوا مِصْرًا

332
00:16:46,100 --> 00:16:48,370
"Why don't you go down to the countryside."

333
00:16:48,400 --> 00:16:51,760
But for countryside, you could use (بَلَد) or (أَرْض) or (قَريَة).

334
00:16:52,150 --> 00:16:56,470
You could use any word. Of all the words Musa (عليه السلام) could have used, he used (مِصْرًا).

335
00:16:56,940 --> 00:16:58,470
What does (مِصْرًا) mean?

336
00:16:59,030 --> 00:17:02,150
Countryside. But it sounds awfully close to what?

337
00:17:03,140 --> 00:17:06,859
Egypt. It's like he said, "Why don't you go into another Egypt - I mean countryside?"

338
00:17:08,040 --> 00:17:09,050
[laughs]

339
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:13,660
And he said that because they're asking for food from Egypt.

340
00:17:14,040 --> 00:17:17,160
So it's like they're asking for a repeat of what happened in

341
00:17:17,640 --> 00:17:18,170
Egypt.

342
00:17:19,040 --> 00:17:22,670
Now let's analyze for a little bit. What is so bad about Egypt?

343
00:17:23,440 --> 00:17:25,660
What is so bad about it? For them being in Egypt?

344
00:17:26,540 --> 00:17:27,260
Three things.

345
00:17:28,339 --> 00:17:30,300
When you're slaves, you're humiliated.

346
00:17:30,580 --> 00:17:31,550
First problem.

347
00:17:32,040 --> 00:17:33,560
It's a humiliating life.

348
00:17:34,740 --> 00:17:37,630
When you're slaves, you are stuck. You cannot -

349
00:17:37,630 --> 00:17:41,890
There's no upward mobility. You cannot say, "At least I was living a poor life,

350
00:17:41,900 --> 00:17:46,560
my children will live a better life. I didn't go to college, my kids will go to college.

351
00:17:47,240 --> 00:17:49,680
I only worked in, you know, entry level.

352
00:17:49,680 --> 00:17:52,120
My children will become managers or own businesses."

353
00:17:52,130 --> 00:17:56,570
If you are slaves, you are stuck and so are your children. And so are their children.

354
00:17:56,570 --> 00:17:58,550
And so are their children. You understand that?

355
00:17:59,140 --> 00:18:02,250
The first problem was humility - or humiliation.

356
00:18:02,390 --> 00:18:03,960
The second problem is you're trapped.

357
00:18:04,340 --> 00:18:05,860
And so are your generations.

358
00:18:07,240 --> 00:18:12,460
The third problem, especially in Egypt, is every other year, what does the king do?

359
00:18:13,540 --> 00:18:18,550
He kills babies. Meaning they are victims of a king's craziness. A king's anger.

360
00:18:19,720 --> 00:18:21,260
They received the king's rage.

361
00:18:22,140 --> 00:18:25,670
Three problems in Egypt. Can I repeat those problems for you? You tell me.

362
00:18:26,040 --> 00:18:27,170
What was the first problem?

363
00:18:27,940 --> 00:18:29,560
Humiliation. What was the second problem?

364
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:31,170
Huh?

365
00:18:32,110 --> 00:18:33,060
They're stuck.

366
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:36,350
Not just them, their future generations too. What's the next problem?

367
00:18:37,640 --> 00:18:39,590
The rage of Fir'awn.

368
00:18:40,240 --> 00:18:42,060
The rage of Fir'awn. Okay.

369
00:18:43,220 --> 00:18:45,270
They ask for the food from Egypt.

370
00:18:46,340 --> 00:18:49,820
Musa says, "Why don't you go down to another Egypt." Countryside, Egypt,

371
00:18:49,830 --> 00:18:50,360
same thing.

372
00:18:52,140 --> 00:18:53,270
Allah says,

373
00:18:53,740 --> 00:18:56,160
فَإِنَّ لَكُم مَّا سَأَلۡتُمۡ

374
00:18:57,240 --> 00:18:59,450
And in it is what you asked for

375
00:19:00,340 --> 00:19:01,060
and then some.

376
00:19:02,040 --> 00:19:03,450
You don't even know what you asked for.

377
00:19:04,140 --> 00:19:07,260
It's like Musa (عليه السلام) is saying, "Well, you asked for it.

378
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:09,560
You asked for it."

379
00:19:10,140 --> 00:19:10,950
So they go in.

380
00:19:11,340 --> 00:19:12,360
And what does Allah say?

381
00:19:12,840 --> 00:19:13,490
Allah says,

382
00:19:13,620 --> 00:19:15,380
وَضُرِبَتۡ عَلَیۡهِمُ ٱلذِّلَّةُ

383
00:19:15,840 --> 00:19:19,950
وَٱلۡمَسۡكَنَةُ وَبَاۤءُو بِغَضَبࣲ مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ

384
00:19:19,980 --> 00:19:25,460
Allah says, the second time around, they were slapped with humiliation.

385
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:29,330
This is after Egypt. They were slapped with humiliation.

386
00:19:29,330 --> 00:19:30,360
Was that a problem in Egypt?

387
00:19:31,140 --> 00:19:34,560
Yes. Then He says they were slapped with being trapped.

388
00:19:34,570 --> 00:19:39,450
Stuck, cannot move out, cannot move up. Was that the problem in Egypt?

389
00:19:39,940 --> 00:19:40,500
Yes.

390
00:19:40,730 --> 00:19:41,236
Then He says,

391
00:19:41,254 --> 00:19:42,916
وَبَاۤءُو بِغَضَبࣲ

392
00:19:42,916 --> 00:19:45,416
And they drew upon themselves rage.

393
00:19:46,540 --> 00:19:49,500
Wait. Was that a problem in Egpyt? They drew rage upon themselves?

394
00:19:49,710 --> 00:19:52,570
But in Egypt, whose rage did they draw upon themselves?

395
00:19:53,040 --> 00:19:54,550
Fir'awn's. This time Allah says,

396
00:19:54,584 --> 00:19:56,730
وَبَاۤءُو بِغَضَبࣲ مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ

397
00:19:56,740 --> 00:19:58,770
They drew upon themselves rage from Allah.

398
00:19:59,940 --> 00:20:00,450
Wow.

399
00:20:01,540 --> 00:20:04,660
In one word Musa said, "You're asking for another Egpyt."

400
00:20:05,040 --> 00:20:08,270
And then Allah describes, "Let me tell you why this was even worse than Egypt."

401
00:20:09,340 --> 00:20:10,660
This was even worse than Egypt.

402
00:20:11,040 --> 00:20:15,520
And that entire sarcastic commentary is captured inside just that one word.

403
00:20:15,530 --> 00:20:18,170
ٱهۡبِطُوا۟ مِصۡرࣰا فَإِنَّ لَكُم مَّا سَأَلۡتُمۡ

404
00:20:18,540 --> 00:20:19,560
SubhaanAllah.

405
00:20:21,040 --> 00:20:23,370
Surah al-Saff, Allah (عز وجل) says,

406
00:20:24,050 --> 00:20:27,621
- He talks about Musa (عليه السلام), then He talks about Isa (عليه السلام).

407
00:20:28,140 --> 00:20:29,550
Aayaat are back to back.

408
00:20:29,940 --> 00:20:31,170
They're back to back aayaat.

409
00:20:32,240 --> 00:20:33,950
Who did Musa (عليه السلام) come to?

410
00:20:34,740 --> 00:20:35,670
Which nation?

411
00:20:36,940 --> 00:20:38,770
The Israelites. Who did Isa come to?

412
00:20:39,940 --> 00:20:40,760
Same nation.

413
00:20:41,030 --> 00:20:42,800
They both were sent to the same nation.

414
00:20:42,850 --> 00:20:44,150
Banu Israa'eel. Yes?

415
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:44,660
Okay.

416
00:20:45,840 --> 00:20:48,477
Now, when Musa spoke to them, Allah says,

417
00:20:48,477 --> 00:20:50,087
وَإِذۡ قَالَ مُوسَىٰ لِقَوۡمِهِ

418
00:20:50,100 --> 00:20:53,060
I want you to just appreciate the English. I think I wrote it down over here.

419
00:20:54,040 --> 00:20:54,960
Look at the English.

420
00:20:56,340 --> 00:20:57,979
When Musa (عليه السلام)

421
00:20:59,440 --> 00:21:02,560
spoke, he addresses them as his nation.

422
00:21:03,640 --> 00:21:07,190
Allah says Musa spoke to his nation.

423
00:21:07,210 --> 00:21:09,610
وَإِذۡ قَالَ مُوسَىٰ لِقَوۡمِهِ

424
00:21:09,610 --> 00:21:11,210
To his nation.

425
00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:12,950
But Allah did not say that for Isa.

426
00:21:13,940 --> 00:21:15,370
وَإِذۡ قَالَ عِیسَى ٱبۡنُ مَرۡیَمَ

427
00:21:15,370 --> 00:21:18,000
Not,
وَإِذۡ قَالَ عِیسَى ٱبۡنُ مَرۡیَمَ لِقَوۡمِهِ

428
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:22,460
"Isa spoke to his nation." So here's the easy comparison, so you can visualize it.

429
00:21:22,840 --> 00:21:25,660
Musa said to his nation.

430
00:21:26,840 --> 00:21:28,160
And Isa

431
00:21:29,850 --> 00:21:31,170
Son of Maryam,

432
00:21:33,540 --> 00:21:34,170
said.

433
00:21:36,540 --> 00:21:37,950
What's missing in the second one?

434
00:21:39,440 --> 00:21:40,270
"To his nation."

435
00:21:41,240 --> 00:21:43,580
And when he spoke to them, he said,

436
00:21:47,440 --> 00:21:48,360
یَـٰقَوۡمِ

437
00:21:49,040 --> 00:21:49,850
My nation!

438
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:51,661
لِمَ تُؤۡذُونَنِی

439
00:21:51,667 --> 00:21:53,871
Why are you torturing me?

440
00:21:53,910 --> 00:21:57,170
That's how he spoke to them. When Isa spoke to them, he said.

441
00:21:58,040 --> 00:21:59,651
یَـٰبَنِیۤ إِسۡرَ ٰ⁠ۤءِیلَ

442
00:21:59,686 --> 00:22:00,496
Sons of Israel!

443
00:22:01,940 --> 00:22:02,960
Sons of Israel!

444
00:22:03,640 --> 00:22:05,050
He did not call them what?

445
00:22:06,140 --> 00:22:06,860
My nation.

446
00:22:07,840 --> 00:22:10,870
The thing is, to be from a nation, your father has to be

447
00:22:11,030 --> 00:22:11,860
from that nation.

448
00:22:13,220 --> 00:22:16,050
To be from a nation, your father has to be from the nation.

449
00:22:16,540 --> 00:22:20,390
Musa (عليه السلام) spoke to them and he said, "My nation."

450
00:22:20,390 --> 00:22:24,760
Which is his way of saying, "I am from you," because who's from you also?

451
00:22:25,340 --> 00:22:27,010
"My father is from you also."

452
00:22:27,840 --> 00:22:30,860
Isa (عليه السلام) will never call them his nation.

453
00:22:32,040 --> 00:22:33,060
Why not?

454
00:22:34,240 --> 00:22:36,160
He doesn't have a father.

455
00:22:36,740 --> 00:22:37,960
He doesn't have a father.

456
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:44,700
So Allah will never call them "his nation". Nor will he ever call them "my nation".

457
00:22:44,710 --> 00:22:47,860
Actually, when he speaks to them, he will say "Sons of Israel"

458
00:22:49,140 --> 00:22:52,850
because he is a unique case. In his case and only his case

459
00:22:52,860 --> 00:22:57,170
his identity does not come from his father. His identity comes from

460
00:22:57,640 --> 00:22:59,330
his mother, and that's unique,

461
00:22:59,340 --> 00:23:02,350
which is why he's the only prophet where you find "ibn something".

462
00:23:03,440 --> 00:23:05,952
Because he's a unique case, so Allah does not only say Isa, He says

463
00:23:05,990 --> 00:23:08,440
"Isa ibn Maryam."

464
00:23:08,490 --> 00:23:10,430
Because that normally doesn't happen. Normally,

465
00:23:10,430 --> 00:23:11,860
where do you get your last name from?

466
00:23:12,840 --> 00:23:16,350
Your father. Isa (عليه السلام) gets his last name from his mother. Isa ibn Maryam.

467
00:23:17,640 --> 00:23:19,950
He gets his last name from his mom because there's no dad involved.

468
00:23:20,540 --> 00:23:21,740
There's no father there.

469
00:23:21,750 --> 00:23:25,850
And so the perfection of the Quran in maintaining the

470
00:23:25,860 --> 00:23:30,930
the virgin birth of Isa (عليه السلام) in
یَـٰبَنِیۤ إِسۡرَ ٰ⁠ۤءِیلَ

471
00:23:30,940 --> 00:23:34,360
It was interesting. I was having a conversation with a rabbi not too long ago,

472
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:37,170
about ethnicity.

473
00:23:38,240 --> 00:23:39,170
On ethnicity.

474
00:23:39,540 --> 00:23:43,060
Because Orthodox Jews and Jewish scholars nowadays,

475
00:23:43,060 --> 00:23:46,670
they believe that to be Jewish, your mother has to be Jewish.

476
00:23:47,540 --> 00:23:48,770
Your father could be from anywhere.

477
00:23:48,770 --> 00:23:51,540
But if your mother - so long as your mother is Jewish, you're Jewish.

478
00:23:51,570 --> 00:23:54,180
In other words, to them, ethnicity does not come from the father.

479
00:23:54,380 --> 00:23:55,550
Ethnicity comes from

480
00:23:56,140 --> 00:23:56,970
the mother.

481
00:23:57,250 --> 00:23:58,270
That's according to them.

482
00:23:59,240 --> 00:24:00,260
I said, "That's interesting.

483
00:24:00,740 --> 00:24:03,460
So are you sure about that?" He goes, "Yep, Absolutely. We're sure."

484
00:24:04,440 --> 00:24:06,060
"So, okay. Well, then

485
00:24:07,440 --> 00:24:09,170
what about Musa?

486
00:24:10,440 --> 00:24:11,700
Musa's kids?"

487
00:24:13,340 --> 00:24:15,460
Because Musa (عليه السلام) left Egypt.

488
00:24:15,840 --> 00:24:17,050
He went to Madyan.

489
00:24:17,390 --> 00:24:18,860
They know that too, they call it Midian.

490
00:24:19,950 --> 00:24:22,040
Madyan according to them is Arab land.

491
00:24:22,220 --> 00:24:23,670
We know also it's Arab land.

492
00:24:24,140 --> 00:24:25,360
He got married there

493
00:24:26,340 --> 00:24:27,660
to an Arab lady.

494
00:24:28,600 --> 00:24:29,670
Then they had kids.

495
00:24:30,740 --> 00:24:32,660
And those kids were born from an Arab woman,

496
00:24:33,740 --> 00:24:35,110
which means Moses,

497
00:24:35,500 --> 00:24:36,560
my rabbi friend,

498
00:24:37,140 --> 00:24:38,550
has Arab kids.

499
00:24:38,940 --> 00:24:39,770
According to you.

500
00:24:43,840 --> 00:24:48,118
And he said, "Wow, that's interesting." [laughs] That's all he said.

501
00:24:49,108 --> 00:24:52,260
I was like, "Why you gotta hate bro? It's just your cousins."

502
00:24:52,940 --> 00:24:54,670
[laughs]

503
00:24:55,340 --> 00:24:56,360
You know?

504
00:24:56,840 --> 00:24:59,270
We have some fun conversations, the rabbi and I.

505
00:24:59,640 --> 00:25:04,060
On that note, I do want to tell you I'm a big believer in not talking about the Jews.

506
00:25:04,070 --> 00:25:06,170
I'm a big believer in talking to the Jews.

507
00:25:07,540 --> 00:25:08,770
I would rather talk to them.

508
00:25:09,940 --> 00:25:10,660
You know why?

509
00:25:11,340 --> 00:25:12,270
Because Allah did.

510
00:25:13,540 --> 00:25:14,860
Allah said,
یَـٰبَنِیۤ إِسۡرَ ٰ⁠ۤءِیلَ

511
00:25:15,940 --> 00:25:16,860
Sons of Israel.

512
00:25:17,330 --> 00:25:18,670
Did he talk about them or to them?

513
00:25:19,540 --> 00:25:20,160
To them.

514
00:25:20,170 --> 00:25:24,410
So if I am a believer in this book, then I better believe in a conversation with Jews.

515
00:25:25,740 --> 00:25:27,790
And I don't believe that I have to debate with them.

516
00:25:27,790 --> 00:25:30,540
And I don't believe that I have to, you know, love them either.

517
00:25:30,540 --> 00:25:33,220
I just believe that I have to have a civil conversation with them.

518
00:25:33,230 --> 00:25:35,460
There's nothing wrong with it. I can disagree with them all I want.

519
00:25:35,840 --> 00:25:37,960
They can disagree with me, too. It's a free world. It's fine.

520
00:25:38,540 --> 00:25:40,960
We should be able to have a conversation. There's nothing wrong with that.

521
00:25:42,440 --> 00:25:45,270
I'll walk into a church. I'll go talk to a minister. No problem.

522
00:25:45,350 --> 00:25:47,170
I'm not there to debate with him. I just wanna talk to him.

523
00:25:48,340 --> 00:25:49,060
I would say, "Hey!

524
00:25:49,540 --> 00:25:50,950
I don't agree with what you're saying, but

525
00:25:51,440 --> 00:25:52,270
let's go get some pizza."

526
00:25:54,440 --> 00:25:54,950
That's fine.

